codemap 9
tour feature-minimap
kind flow
author ai
group features/gui
note The human reading a tour in the Graph tab cannot see how big the graph is or where the camera sits in it, and reaching a far part of a large graph costs many gestures. Seen in a run: the default view of the anchor tour shows one node in the middle of a large empty canvas, its callees off to the right and nothing saying so; reader-gui (100 steps) is about 33 canvas heights tall at 100% (its minimap shows the camera as 32 of 150 pixels at 14%), so reading its last steps at a readable zoom means some 30 background drags, and fitted it stops at 14% because the graph font cannot shrink further (TODO.md GUI 2) with its lower half still off the canvas. What codemap has does not answer it: pan-graph moves a canvas height per drag and shows nothing of where the view is; zoom-graph and fit-graph show more of the shape but give up the zoom the reader reads at, cannot show a tall tree whole, and need a zoom back in at the right spot afterwards; walk-graph moves one node at a time; the Tour tab orders the steps but not the graph's layout. The minimap serves the goal of reading an unfamiliar flow with less friction than an editor, which has no picture of a call tree at all: the whole graph drawn small in the canvas's bottom-right corner, nodes as boxes without text, the camera as a rectangle on it; one press or drag moves the camera any distance and keeps the zoom. One design was built (the owner asked for no prototypes this time); its choices: an overlay in the canvas rather than a panel, since it is only useful beside the graph it maps; the bottom-right corner above the zoom cluster, where map and design apps put both, never covering the cluster; boxes without text, since text at that scale is what GUI 2 says cannot be read; a world that joins the graph with the camera's view, so the camera is on the minimap even after panning away from the graph, and that stays fixed while the minimap is dragged, so the mapping does not move under the pointer; a press inside the camera rectangle keeps its offset so the view does not jump, a press elsewhere centres there; hidden while the whole graph is on screen, where it would only cover nodes. It moves the camera because pressing it is explicit navigation (CLAUDE.md). It does not settle GUI 2: the graph itself still cannot fit a very tall tree; the minimap is a way to move around one.

